# SECTION A — PRODUCT IDENTITY AND PROJECT OBJECTIVE

## A1. Product name

Application name: Finora

Full project name: Finora — Personal Finance Tracker

Tagline: Track smarter. Plan better. Grow confidently.

The name Finora must appear consistently in the browser title, login and registration screens, dashboard header, sidebar branding, application metadata, and README.

Use a professional financial-product identity. Avoid generic templates that look like an unfinished student dashboard.

Do not rename the application later unless explicitly instructed.

## A2. Product objective

Finora helps individual users manage their personal finances by recording income and expenses, reviewing their financial position, analyzing spending patterns, and managing budgets.

The complete product will eventually include:

1. Secure registration and login.
2. A personalized financial dashboard.
3. Income and expense management.
4. Transaction history, searching, sorting, and filtering.
5. Income, expense, and balance summaries.
6. Category-wise spending charts.
7. Monthly income and expense analytics.
8. Budget management and spending alerts.
9. Recurring transactions.
10. Savings goals.
11. CSV and PDF reports.
12. User profile and preferences.
13. Optional monthly reports and installable PWA support.

However, this milestone must not pretend that all these modules are already implemented.

Implement the foundation and authentication completely, establish the dashboard structure, and display the remaining unimplemented modules as Coming Soon.

The architecture must make future development possible without rewriting the entire application.

## A3. Development philosophy

Follow these non-negotiable rules throughout this task and all subsequent development prompts.

RULE 1: FRONTEND AND BACKEND TOGETHER

For each feature, implement its UI, user interactions, API endpoints, backend validation, database integration where applicable, error handling, loading states, and successful end-to-end flow together.

RULE 2: NO STATIC FUNCTIONALITY CLAIMS

Do not use fake API responses, hardcoded successful logins, simulated database persistence, fake financial totals, or fake transaction records in the real application.

A design preview may use clearly labelled illustrative data, but the actual authenticated application must use real backend responses.

RULE 3: COMING SOON FOR UNIMPLEMENTED MODULES

Every planned module visible on the dashboard must provide a meaningful interaction.

If the module is not implemented, clicking it must open a polished Coming Soon page, panel, or modal that explains that the feature is planned and provides a Back to Dashboard action.

Do not show a fake transaction list, pretend that a report was downloaded, or claim that a budget was saved when the relevant feature does not exist.

RULE 4: NO SILENT FAILURES

Every form submission and user-triggered asynchronous action must show an appropriate loading state and either a visible success result or a useful error message.

Never swallow an error and leave the user wondering whether the action worked.

RULE 5: PRESERVE EXISTING FUNCTIONALITY

Before changing files, inspect the existing repository and understand its architecture.

Do not blindly replace configuration files, package manifests, authentication logic, database connections, routing, or environment variables.

If this repository is already partially implemented, reuse working components and improve them safely. Do not create duplicate applications or competing authentication systems.

RULE 6: NO DESTRUCTIVE DATABASE OPERATIONS

Never drop a database, delete existing production records, reset a database, remove collections, or overwrite user records to make the implementation easier.

Use safe, additive database changes when necessary.

RULE 7: FEATURE IS NOT COMPLETE UNTIL VERIFIED

A successful build alone does not prove that registration, login, session persistence, API connectivity, or database persistence works.

Verify the actual workflows and report exactly what was tested, what passed, and what still requires manual configuration.

RULE 8: MAINTAIN A CONSISTENT THEME

Use the Finora design system described below throughout the entire application.

Do not switch randomly to a white-and-red theme, navy-and-cyan theme, or unrelated colors.

RULE 9: FUTURE FEATURES MUST NOT BREAK EXISTING FEATURES

Use reusable components, typed interfaces where supported, clear API contracts, and modular architecture.

After each change, run the relevant tests and build checks. Fix regressions before reporting completion.

RULE 10: IMPLEMENT, DO NOT JUST EXPLAIN

Do not respond with a list of suggested changes while leaving the application unchanged.

Make the actual code changes using the available project-editing capabilities.

If something cannot be completed because a required service, credential, or permission is unavailable, implement the portions that can be completed, explain the exact blocker, and give the precise manual setup instructions.

