# SECTION E — AUTHENTICATION: COMPLETE FRONTEND AND BACKEND IMPLEMENTATION

Authentication is the first complete functional feature.

Build registration, login, logout, session restoration, protected routes, validation, error handling, and user-specific dashboard access.

Do not create a login page that only looks functional.

## E1. Registration page

Create a premium registration page with:

- Finora branding.
- A concise welcome heading.
- A short description of the application.
- Full name input.
- Email input.
- Password input.
- Confirm password input.
- Show/hide password control.
- Password requirements.
- Terms acknowledgement if required by the existing product policy.
- Create Account button.
- Link to the existing-user login page.
- Loading and error states.

Validate required fields and email format.

Validate that the password meets the defined password policy.

Validate that the password and confirmation match.

Prevent duplicate submissions while a request is in progress.

Use both client-side and server-side validation. Client-side validation must never be the only protection.

When registration succeeds:

- Show a real success message.
- Establish the correct authentication state.
- Redirect to the dashboard when the registration flow permits immediate authenticated access.
- If email verification is required by the selected provider, show the correct verification-pending state instead of pretending the account is already verified.

If registration fails because the email is already registered, show an understandable message.

Do not expose raw database errors, internal stack traces, or secret configuration details to the user.

## E2. Login page

Create a polished login screen with:

- Finora logo and name.
- Welcome-back heading.
- Email field.
- Password field.
- Show/hide password control.
- Remember-me option only if its behavior is genuinely implemented.
- Forgot-password link.
- Sign In button.
- Link to registration.
- Form validation.
- Loading state.
- Inline error messages or an accessible alert.
- Responsive layout.

A user must be able to enter valid credentials and actually sign in.

When the login request succeeds:

- Store or establish the authenticated session using the selected authentication strategy.
- Retrieve the authenticated user's profile.
- Redirect to the protected dashboard.
- Display the correct user identity.
- Load data only for that authenticated user.

When credentials are invalid, display a useful generic error without exposing sensitive account details.

When the backend is unreachable, display an appropriate connection error.

Never redirect to the dashboard after an unsuccessful login.

Never display a success toast merely because the Sign In button was clicked.

## E3. Backend authentication

Implement the appropriate backend routes for the selected authentication architecture.

For a custom JWT implementation, the minimum routes should include:

- POST /api/auth/register
- POST /api/auth/login
- GET /api/auth/me
- POST /api/auth/logout if the session architecture requires a server-side logout endpoint

Add a password-reset flow only if it can be implemented with the required secure token and delivery configuration. Otherwise, show a clearly explained Coming Soon state rather than a fake reset success.

Adapt route names to the existing API conventions if necessary.

The backend must:

- Validate request bodies.
- Normalize email addresses consistently.
- Enforce unique account identities.
- Hash passwords using a suitable password-hashing algorithm and library.
- Never store plain-text passwords.
- Never return password hashes in API responses.
- Sign JWTs using a strong server-side secret when using JWT.
- Verify token signature and expiration.
- Validate the authenticated identity for protected routes.
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Use a consistent error response structure.
- Avoid logging passwords, tokens, or sensitive authentication data.

If the project uses Clerk or another existing authentication provider, use its official authentication and token-verification mechanism instead of creating an unnecessary competing login system.

Do not run two independent authentication systems for the same user without a documented and tested reason.

## E4. Authentication state and session restoration

Implement a single reliable authentication state source.

The frontend must distinguish between:

- Initial authentication loading.
- Authenticated user.
- Unauthenticated user.
- Authentication error.

Do not briefly show protected dashboard content while authentication is still being checked.

On a page refresh, the application should restore the session if the authentication strategy supports persistence.

If the session is invalid or expired, clear invalid client-side state and redirect the user to login.

If using JWTs, carefully evaluate secure cookie storage versus other token-storage approaches. Prefer appropriately configured HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies where compatible with the architecture. If bearer tokens are required, document the chosen storage and its security trade-offs. Do not expose authentication secrets in source code.

If using cookie-based authentication, configure credentialed requests, CORS, cookie attributes, and CSRF protections as appropriate.

Logout must clear or invalidate the actual session using the selected architecture, clear user-specific cached state, and return the user to login.

After logout, manually opening a protected URL must not expose private data.

## E5. Protected API access

Every protected backend route must authenticate the caller.

Do not trust a user ID sent in a request body as proof of ownership.

Derive the authenticated identity from the verified session or token.

For future financial routes, use the authenticated user identity to scope all queries.

Implement a reusable authentication middleware rather than duplicating token parsing logic across route handlers.

